Maybe putting in a small weight to mimic having powder in. Raw vs. stable reading. As a function of lots of different combinations of the dispense parameters.

This ties into the fact that the stepper motor causes vibration. Similarly, this is a calibration that might need to be taken for each new environment (e.g., the glovebox which had a lot of vibration).

@claude comment on above

@claude we need to gather data on the powder doser while it is connected to the scale. We want to find statistical data on how much powder comes out per rotation of the auger and per tap. We will need this data at each angle (or given increments), and multiple data points would need to be collected at each angle. We would need to collect uncertainty data at each angle as well. 

I was thinking that we could have an easily editable variable for the number of data points to collect at each angle. The powder doser would then rotate this number of times, followed by tapping this number of times at each angle. Enough time needs to pass between each tap and rotation to get an accurate scale reading. Taps will most likely dispense less and less powder each time if there is no rotation happening in between each tap, so have the auger rotate between taps and account for the mass displaced during the rotations. 

We need data to be stored somewhere, look at #126 for what we plan to use. Outline what we need to do on our end to ensure the data is uploaded.

Now that we're getting much closer (see #100), we can start thinking more about data. In particular, I think it would be worth it to have the data streaming near-continuously and being captured long-term. MongoDB has that time-series version, not sure how far 500 MB would get us. Likewise, other issues that come with streaming.

@claude see https://github.com/AccelerationConsortium/ac-dev-lab/issues/113, https://huggingface.co/spaces/AccelerationConsortium/scale-monitor, and there's probably a PR or branch associated with https://github.com/AccelerationConsortium/ac-dev-lab/issues/113 that has the code, it's not linked directly though. I'm assuming it got merged and lives here though: https://github.com/AccelerationConsortium/ac-dev-lab/tree/main/src/ac_training_lab/picow/autotrickler-scale, though there wasn't a MongoDB data connection involved there, it was just streaming. Consider the setup in https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/powder-doser/pull/100 and various other PRs/branches in terms of the latest version of code being run

I also want a link to create a PR from you

@seth-leavitt realizing that a second camera is now much more relevant 😅 perhaps you can just take the camera that I mentioned bringing over to the OT-2, instead to where they've been doing the powder dosing recently. You'd want to check with @swcharles @williamulbz or @lbwinters for where it would be best positioned.

Noting for @swcharles @williamulbz and @lbwinters this won't replace recording videos (these streams will be large, 8 hr chunks, with no audio, and poorer resolution, but serve as a nice long-term provenant resource that can be checked on -- it's also something that can be used to help with remote development, since we'll get a device running a full OS that we can SSH into and that connects to the Pico W, Claude can probably check the streams for example)

@seth-leavitt please set this to public, but work with Sam, Will, or Luke to make sure that it's oriented in a way that's unlikely to capture people's faces (just hands/up to elbow)

@copilot suggest methods for measuring the maximum possible volume of each auger, focusing on Auger Type 4 as described [here](https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/powder-doser/issues/48#issuecomment-4513155870). Also, select the best method and calculate powder capacity for Auger 4.

One suggested method is using cad tools, but the engineers on this project are trying to use cad software as little as possible, so avoid that solution if possible.

If possible, this measurement would be a function of length, i.e. we would know that a 5in auger has x powder capacity

@copilot we want this issue to be a record of every design we have collectively made so far in this repository, i.e. any design you have made/iterated based on a user comment or review, etc. This includes every commit, every design after every comment, every issue and pull request, etc. Go through all the activity in this repository from its inception and create a file (or several) here to chronologically document all designs from this repository. This means you will have to go through every issue, pull request, comment, and commit, etc. We want it chronological, not project based, i.e. make comments for and list all designs in order of creation, don't put all augers together or all mounting plates together. This can be done in one big markdown file, or however else you think is prudent. However, it should be scrollable and easily viewable, i.e. separate files for each design would be bad, so one markdown file is probably best.

Make one comment/entry for each design. Each entry should include 1-3 good visuals of the design and a 1-2 sentence summary of the design, what changed, and why the changes were made (based on the comments and/or review that started the new session and design). This can include diagrams and even drawing I've supplied but should specifically include render views to accurately communicate designs. This will be a lot of comments and a lot of images, which is the point--a full record we can scroll through to quickly and simply see the progress of design throughout this process. We recognize that this will include many repetitions of objects, for example, while we only have one issue/PR for the bracket design, I want every iteration of the bracket design on here, placed correctly in the chronological list of when they were created.

If for any reason you are unable to complete this request, return a short but detailed explanation why.

@copilot I don't like this statement from https://github.com/vertical-cloud-lab/powder-excavator/pull/2#issuecomment-4309337047

> chosen over Rhino/Grasshopper, Fusion Generative Design, nTop, Onshape FeatureScript because it's pure-Python and pip-installable — none of the others survive the "freshly-cloned repo on a CI runner" test

You have a full dev environment. You should try to install each of these and if you really can't do anything meaningful within a 60-minute session, that's one thing - but show me. I want you to look at the context in that PR and not be so restricted to Python-only and pip-installable. It doesn't need to be Python necessarily, but it would be nice
